{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3cb","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a811-e233-7700-9f73-d0f94294967e"},"sequence":64,"recorded_at":1780531400000123,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d4ea5ec8-93af-4409-aa5b-edfe86e8078d","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"d4ea5ec8-93af-4409-aa5b-edfe86e8078d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d4ea5ec8-93af-4409-aa5b-edfe86e8078d"},"call_id":"call_01a0a8120e2570b190056073432808f8","text":"Read text file `RELEASING.md`.\n1|# Release operations\n2|\n3|## One-time setup on macbook-iv\n4|\n5|The release workflow uses this Mac's manually controlled runner. Enable it in Runner Control before releasing; a disabled runner leaves the workflow queued. Pull requests run unit tests on GitHub-hosted macOS and do not use release credentials.\n6|\n7|Required tools: Xcode, Tuist 4.9.0, GitHub CLI, Python 3. The workflow installs Go 1.25.5 and builds the scaffold generator at a pinned revision. Sparkle tools are downloaded at a pinned version and verified by SHA-256.\n8|\n9|The runner's login Keychain needs:\n10|\n11|- A Developer ID Application certificate **and matching private key** for team `262RZ595FP`. Apple Development and certificates belonging to other teams are rejected.\n12|- A validated notarytool profile named `RunnerControl-notary`.\n13|- The existing Sparkle Ed25519 signing key under account `works.relux.runnercontrol`. Keep a secure backup; losing it prevents updates to installed releases. Do not regenerate it for each release.\n14|\n15|An Apple Account Holder must issue the Developer ID certificate from the supplied CSR. Import the issued certificate together with its matching private key. Use `xcrun notarytool store-credentials` locally with a valid App Store Connect API key, key ID and issuer ID; it validates authorization before saving the profile. Never commit keys, certificates with private keys, or credentials.\n16|\n17|Run `./Scripts/release-preflight.sh` from any directory to verify these prerequisites. Keychain access must work from the runner session without an unattended authorization prompt.\n18|\n19|## Publish\n20|\n21|1. Set `marketing_version` in `ios-app-manager.json` to the intended version and regenerate with the pinned macOS app generator.\n22|2. Commit to `main` and wait for the Tests workflow.\n23|3. Tag that commit `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` and push the tag. Alternatively run **Release DMG** with that existing tag.\n24|4. The workflow tests, archives, exports with Developer ID, notarizes/staples the app and DMG, generates the Sparkle signature, and checks feed metadata.\n25|5. Only after those checks does it create a draft release, upload `RunnerControl.dmg`, `appcast.xml` and `SHA256SUMS`, then publish it as latest. Assets remain in GitHub Releases without the artifact retention deadline.\n26|\n27|Each feed points to its immutable version-specific DMG URL. The application follows the latest release's appcast. Published release assets are never overwritten by the workflow. If uploading fails after draft creation, inspect the draft and remove the incomplete draft before retrying the same run. Diagnostic logs are retained for 14 days.\n28|\n29|Build numbers are derived from the release workflow run number and attempt. Keep this workflow's run counter continuous; do not replace it with a new workflow whose counter starts over without adjusting the build-number scheme.\n30|\n31|## Failure recovery\n32|\n33|Release run 35040921163 (`v1.1.0`) failed in Release prerequisites before any\n34|build or signing: the login keychain was locked in the runner session, so\n35|`notarytool` could not read profile `RunnerControl-notary` (`keychainLocked`).\n36|No draft or published release was created.\n37|\n38|To retry an existing tag, unlock the login keychain on macbook-iv with a\n39|console login as the runner user (no credentials are printed or exported),\n40|enable the runner in Runner Control, then run **Release DMG** via\n41|workflow_dispatch with the existing tag. Do not delete or re-push the tag and\n42|do not create the release by hand; the workflow refuses to overwrite published\n43|assets. If prerequisites still report a locked keychain, the runner session\n44|has no unlocked login keychain; unlock it and dispatch again.\n45|\n46|Preflight also fails fast when no Developer ID Application identity for team\n47|`262RZ595FP` is present, when several are present (remove the stale\n48|certificate so signing is unambiguous), when the notarytool profile is missing\n49|(recreate it with `xcrun notarytool store-credentials` as above), or when the\n50|Sparkle private key does not match `SUPublicEDKey`.\n51|\n52|Apple notarization waits up to 30 minutes per artifact (app ZIP, then DMG). If\n53|a wait times out, the submission ID in the release logs can be inspected with\n54|`xcrun notarytool info <id> --keychain-profile RunnerControl-notary` (status\n55|only); retrying dispatches a new run with a new monotonic build number. A\n56|pending signing-check submission is not a release artifact and does not block\n57|the next release.\n58|\n59|No new tag is created and nothing is published until product review and\n60|coordinator dispatch.\n61|\n62|## Validation status\n63|\n64|Core and release metadata tests pass locally and in GitHub Actions; the Sparkle-enabled app builds successfully. Developer ID signing and the notarytool profile are validated on macbook-iv. The complete notarization and update-install cycle requires the first accepted Apple submission and two published versions. Check GitHub Releases for publication status.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
